{
  "_comment": "Plan 002 §7 P9 — language-neutral credential.response vectors. Every vector is a frame as an agent writes it plus the ARM a client must map it to, where the arm IS the mode (token | certificate | refused). The refusals are the point: an unknown auth_mode must NEVER fall back to the bearer-token arm (that is the silent downgrade the mtls rollout exists to prevent), the mode string is matched EXACTLY (no trimming, no case folding — mirroring Go sdk.Bundle.Mode's \"case does not fuzzy-match\" rule), a frame carrying both credential shapes (including a bare cert_serial on a token answer) is a protocol violation rather than an empty success, a POPULATED but unparseable expires_at refuses instead of silently becoming \"no expiry\", and every field has a size cap (`limits`). `expires_at_unix` is the parsed expiry (null when the frame carries none). A vector with `oversize_field` has no literal frame value for that field: build it as `limits.<field>_bytes + 1` repetitions of `oversize_char`, so a 4 KiB string doesn't live in this file. `agent_emits` marks the vectors a SHIPPING agent build actually produces, END TO END: both agent suites drive their REAL `credential.get` handler over its real socket, with a minter returning that vector's credential, and require the emitted frame to be these exact bytes. Its absence means the vector describes something only a CLIENT decodes. That includes shapes an agent CANNOT emit however the mint went: `auth_mode` is normalized to the `token`/`mtls` literals by `sdk.Bundle.Mode()` (Go) and `ControlTokenProvider::mint_control_credential` (Rust), so `secure` and an absent mode are unreachable — and an agent old enough to have meant them predates `credential.get` itself, so it answers nothing at all. Likewise `server` is echoed from the request, so it is never absent or mismatched.",
  "protocol_version": 1,
  "server": "prod",
  "limits": {
    "token_bytes": 4096,
    "client_cert_bytes": 65536,
    "cert_serial_bytes": 128,
    "error_bytes": 4096,
    "csr_bytes": 16384
  },
